{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Gurley (population around 1,000) sits at the junction of Highway 72 and the foothills leading to Monte Sano. It's the kind of small town where people know their neighbors, property lots are generous, and the views of the mountains make every morning worth it.</p>

<p>But that geography creates real challenges for home drainage:</p>

<p><strong>Slope and gravity.</strong> Many Gurley properties sit on terrain that slopes toward the house, away from the house, or across the house. Standard gutter installations designed for flat lots often fail here. Water accelerates on slopes, which means gutters need more capacity and downspouts need to handle greater flow rates. We often specify 6-inch gutters instead of standard 5-inch, and 3x4 downspouts instead of 2x3.</p>

<p><strong>Tree coverage.</strong> The mixed hardwood and pine forests surrounding Gurley are beautiful — and brutal on gutters. Fall brings oak and hickory leaves by the bushel. But pines are the bigger problem: they shed needles year-round, and those needles slip through standard gutter screens. Micro-mesh guards with smaller openings are essential for most Gurley properties.</p>

<p><strong>Foundation exposure.</strong> On hillside lots, foundations are often partially exposed on the downhill side. This makes them more vulnerable to water damage when drainage systems fail. Getting water routed properly isn't just about convenience — it's about protecting a critical structural element of your home.</p>

<p><strong>Access challenges.</strong> Some Gurley properties have steep driveways or limited access that makes gutter work more complicated. We're equipped to handle these situations and factor them into our estimates upfront.</p>

<p>Whether you're on Highway 72, tucked into the foothills near Monte Sano, or out toward Paint Rock Valley, we've <a href="/recent-projects/">worked on properties like yours</a>. We know what works.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Quick reference for Gurley property owners:</p>

<p><strong>Know your slope.</strong> After a heavy rain, walk your property. Where does water flow? Where does it pool? If it's heading toward your foundation or concentrating in problem areas, your drainage system needs adjustment.</p>

<p><strong>Size matters more here.</strong> Standard 5-inch gutters and 2x3 downspouts may not cut it on sloped properties. When water accelerates downhill, you need more capacity to capture and route it properly.</p>

<p><strong>Gutter guards are essential, not optional.</strong> With Gurley's tree coverage, unprotected gutters will clog repeatedly. The right guards — specifically micro-mesh for pine needle areas — dramatically reduce maintenance while keeping your system functional.</p>

<p><strong>Underground drains solve hillside problems.</strong> When standard downspout extensions dump water that just flows back toward your foundation, underground drainage pipes can route it safely away. This is common for <a href="/recent-projects/">properties in our area</a> with significant grade changes.</p>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season inspection — make sure everything flows before spring rains</li>
<li><strong>June:</strong> Mid-summer check — storms may have deposited debris</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> The big fall cleaning after hardwood leaf drop</li>
<li><strong>January:</strong> Quick check before freeze potential — ice dams start with clogged gutters</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Signs you need help:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Water overflowing during moderate rain (not just heavy storms)</li>
<li>Erosion channels forming on slopes</li>
<li>Pooling water near foundation, especially on the downhill side</li>
<li>Gutters visibly sagging or pulling away from fascia</li>
<li>Staining on siding below gutter line</li>
</ul>

